L.marker([{lat},{lon}]).addTo(m);</script>""", height=500)
    st.caption("Latest available NASA GIBS imagery, for visual context (not used in the scores). If a layer looks blank, try another layer or zoom out. "
               "Green on the NDVI layer = more vegetation; soil moisture has gaps where there is no recent satellite pass.")

# ---------------- 7. Validation ----------------
with tabs[6]:
    st.subheader("Did the NASA data 'see' real drought seasons?")
    st.caption("Sanity check against documented events (not a yield validation). Season 2023 = the 2023/24 rainy season (Oct 2023 - Apr 2024).")
    dv = pd.DataFrame(series_all).set_index("season")
    mz = [v3.season_score(x, crops["maize"], soil, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0) for x in series_all]
    dv["maize_score"], dv["maize_ok"] = [m[0] for m in mz], [m[1] for m in mz]
    avg, rows = dv["total"].mean(), []
    for sea, lab in ((2015, "2015/16 El Niño drought"), (2023, "2023/24 El Niño drought (national disaster, 29 Feb 2024)")):
        if sea in dv.index:
            r = dv.loc[sea]
            rows.append({"Season": lab, "Rain (mm)": round(r["total"]), "vs average": f"{(r['total'] / avg - 1) * 100:+.0f}%",
                         "Dryness rank (1 = driest)": f"{int(dv['total'].rank(method='min')[sea])} of {len(dv)}",
                         "Longest dry spell (days)": int(r["spell"]), "Maize would have worked?": "Yes" if r["maize_ok"] else "No"})
    st.dataframe(pd.DataFrame(rows), hide_index=True)
    mark = dv.index.isin([2015, 2023])
    st.bar_chart(pd.DataFrame({"Maize season score": dv["maize_score"].where(~mark), "Known drought seasons": dv["maize_score"].where(mark)}))
    st.markdown("""**What was reported:** Zambia declared the 2023/24 drought a national disaster; about 1.1 million of 2.2 million hectares of planted maize were affected
(84 of 116 districts); the Agriculture Minister said maize output was projected to fall from 3.26 to 1.51 million tonnes, with Southern, Western and Eastern provinces
hit hardest. The 2015/16 El Niño drought was described as the worst in Southern Africa in about 35 years.

**How to read this:** if the NASA-derived rainfall for the selected site ranks these seasons among the driest, the data agrees with the reports. In sites that were less
affected (for example the wetter north), a weaker signal is also consistent with the reported pattern. Try Choma and Mongu, then Kasama.""")

