with tabs[7]:
    st.subheader("How FieldShift answers the challenge")
    st.dataframe(pd.DataFrame([
        ["Use NASA Earth observations", "NASA POWER climate (25 yr), NASA GIBS imagery (MODIS, SMAP) in the satellite tab"],
        ["Local soil information", "Texture, organic matter, drainage, acidity and slope change the scores"],
        ["Crop characteristics", "Eight editable crop trait profiles (water, temperature, nitrogen, residue, cost, market)"],
        ["Farmer priorities", "Five priority sliders weight the ranking"],
        ["Explore rotation strategies", "Ranked 2-4 season plans, build-your-own comparison, stress tests"],
        ["Strengthen soil health", "Soil health score: nitrogen, residue, cover, variety, disease break"],
        ["Adapt to changing conditions", "Climate trends, what-if sliders, drier/hotter/erratic stress scenarios"]],
        columns=["Challenge requirement", "Where in FieldShift"]), hide_index=True)
    st.subheader("What each part of the result is based on")
    st.dataframe(pd.DataFrame([
        ["Rainfall, temperature, dry spells, rain onset", "NASA POWER daily data, 2001-2025", "NASA"],
        ["Root-zone soil wetness chart", "NASA POWER (model-derived)", "NASA"],
        ["Satellite imagery (true color, NDVI, SMAP soil moisture)", "NASA GIBS, visual context only", "NASA"],
        ["Soil texture, organic matter, drainage, acidity, slope", "Entered by the farmer", "Farmer"],
        ["Last crop and priorities", "Entered by the farmer", "Farmer"],
        ["Crop water, temperature, nitrogen, residue, cost, market traits", "Placeholders, editable; verify with FAO EcoCrop/GAEZ", "Placeholder"],
        ["Rotation, resilience and soil-health scores", "Transparent rules computed by FieldShift", "Model"]],
        columns=["Part", "Source", "Type"]), hide_index=True)
    st.markdown("""
**How scoring works:** each rotation gets five 0-100% scores. *Soil health* combines nitrogen balance, crop residue, ground cover
(counted more on slopes), crop variety and disease break (residue counted more on low-organic-matter soil). *Resilience* checks the
rotation against every past season and against drier, hotter, dry-and-hot and erratic-rain stress cases. *Income*, *low input cost* and
*food security* use relative crop traits. Your priority sliders weight the five scores into the overall ranking.

**Limits:** scores are relative indices, not yield or price forecasts. Climate data is area-level and coarser than one plot. Soil is farmer-entered.
Cassava is a long-season crop; treating it as one season is a simplification. Trend statements over about 24 seasons are indicative. Crop traits need verification before real-world use. Always confirm with local extension officers.

**Next steps:** add SMAP and IMERG time series and NDVI-based field vigor, ISRIC SoilGrids for soil defaults, local prices, and farmer testing.
""")
